<MarolaDoc
  title="Marola — Privacy Policy"
  description="How Marola, the water sports gear rental app, collects, uses and protects your data."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
>
  <p class="doc-lead">
    Marola connects people who rent water sports gear with the riders, schools and shops who
    lend it. To do that it has to know who you are, what you list and book, and what you say to
    the people you rent with. This page explains exactly what that is, who else handles it, and
    how to get it deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    Marola is operated by David Ferreira, based in Portugal, who is the controller of your
    personal data for the purposes of the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). You can
    reach me at <a href="mailto:hey@davidferreira.dev?subject=Marola%20privacy">hey@davidferreira.dev</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What Marola collects</h2>
  <h3>Your account</h3>
  <p>
    Your email address and a display name, from the way you sign in: Sign in with Apple, Google,
    or a one-time code sent to your email. If you use Sign in with Apple and hide your email,
    Marola only sees Apple's relay address.
  </p>
  <h3>Your listings</h3>
  <p>
    What you describe about your gear, its photos, price, deposit and availability, and where it
    can be picked up. Photos are resized and stripped of their metadata on your phone before
    upload, so the location, time and device details a camera records are never sent. Your
    listing shows an approximate area, rounded to about a kilometre. The exact pickup address is
    shared only with a renter whose booking you have accepted.
  </p>
  <h3>Your bookings and payments</h3>
  <p>
    The gear, dates, prices, fees, deposits and status of every booking you make or receive.
    Card payments are handled by Stripe: Marola never sees or stores your card number. Marola
    keeps a record of each payment's amount and status. If you lend gear, Stripe collects the
    identity and bank details it needs to pay you out, under its own terms.
  </p>
  <h3>Your conversations and handovers</h3>
  <p>
    Messages and photos you send to other users, the photo checks you take at pickup and return,
    reviews you write, and reports you file. Handover photos are kept with the rental record and
    are visible to both people on that rental, because they are the evidence if something goes
    wrong.
  </p>
  <h3>Business details</h3>
  <p>
    If you set up a school or shop profile: your business name, type and website, which are
    public, and any legal name or tax identifier you provide, which are not.
  </p>
  <h3>Your device</h3>
  <p>
    A push notification token, if you turn notifications on. Crash reports stay on your device.
  </p>

  <h2>What Marola does not do</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>No advertising, and no advertising identifiers.</li>
    <li>No tracking across other apps or websites, and no analytics SDKs.</li>
    <li>No selling or renting your data to anyone.</li>
    <li>No access to your contacts, and no background location. The only location Marola has is the pickup point an owner chooses to enter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is used</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>To provide the service</strong> (performance of a contract): your account, listings, bookings, payments, messages, handover records and notifications.</li>
    <li><strong>To keep the marketplace safe</strong> (legitimate interests): reviewing reports, blocking abuse, resolving damage disputes, and preventing fraud.</li>
    <li><strong>To meet legal obligations</strong>: keeping payment and booking records for accounting and tax purposes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who else handles it</h2>
  <p>Marola relies on a small number of service providers, each processing data only to run the service:</p>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Supabase</strong> hosts Marola's database, file storage and sign-in.</li>
    <li><strong>Stripe</strong> processes payments, deposits and owner payouts, under <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">Stripe's privacy policy</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Apple</strong> and <strong>Google</strong> provide sign-in if you choose them, and Apple delivers push notifications.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some of these providers process data outside the European Economic Area, including in the
    United States. Where they do, the transfer is covered by the European Commission's Standard
    Contractual Clauses or the EU–US Data Privacy Framework.
  </p>
  <p>
    Other Marola users see what the service needs them to: your display name, rating, listings
    and reviews publicly; your messages and handover photos only on rentals you share with them;
    your exact pickup address only once you've accepted their booking.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    Your data is kept while your account is open. When you delete your account, your login,
    email, avatar, listings, listing photos, pickup addresses, saved gear, notification tokens
    and business details are removed. Records of rentals you took part in, and the payments,
    messages, reviews and handover photos attached to them, are kept so the other person still
    has their record and so Marola can meet its accounting obligations. Your name on them is
    replaced with “Deleted user”.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
  <p>
    Open Marola, go to Profile, and tap <strong>Delete account</strong>. It happens straight
    away. If you have a rental in progress, finish or cancel it first. Requests that nobody has
    answered yet are withdrawn for you. You can also email me to ask for deletion.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask for a copy of your data, ask for it to be corrected or deleted, object to or ask
    to restrict how it is used, and ask for it in a portable format. Email <a href="mailto:hey@davidferreira.dev?subject=Marola%20privacy">hey@davidferreira.dev</a> and
    I'll respond within a month. If you're not satisfied, you can complain to the Portuguese data
    protection authority, the <a href="https://www.cnpd.pt">CNPD</a>, or the authority where you
    live.
  </p>

  <h2>Age</h2>
  <p>
    Marola is for adults. You must be 18 or older to create an account, because renting and
    lending involves payments and binding agreements.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be published here with a new date at the top,
    and significant changes will be announced in the app.
  </p>
</MarolaDoc>
